====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[diary]] or daily record of a person, organization, vessel etc.; [[daybook]].
# A [[newspaper]] or [[magazine]] dealing with a particular subject.
#: {{ux|en|The university's biology department subscribes to half a dozen academic '''journals'''.}}
# {{lb|en|accounting}} A chronological record of payments or receipts.
# {{lb|en|accounting}} A {{w|general journal}}.
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[chronological]] [[record]] of [[change]]s made to a [[database]] or other system; along with a [[backup]] or [[image copy]] that allows [[recovery]] after a failure or [[reinstatement]] to a previous time; a [[log]].

=====Usage notes=====
* In the sense “daily record”, {{m|en|diary}} and '''journal''' are often used interchangeably. When usage is distinguished, ''diary'' refers more strictly to a personal daily record, while '''journal''' is used more loosely, for example for less frequent, thematic personal writing.<ref>{{cite-web |title=What’s the difference between “diary” and “journal”? |titleurl=https://english.stackexchange.com/questions/70519/whats-the-difference-between-diary-and-journal |work=English Language &amp; Usage |date=2012-06-10|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260104160916/https://english.stackexchange.com/questions/70519/whats-the-difference-between-diary-and-journal|archivedate=4 January 2026}}</ref>

